{
  "id": "l3",
  "number": 3,
  "title": "Mistä sä oot?",
  "subtitle": "Where are you from?",
  "tags": [
    "basics",
    "places",
    "origins",
    "cases"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "intro",
      "text": "You can introduce yourself. Now — where are you from? Finnish has a wonderfully logical way of expressing this. Once you see the pattern, it practically builds itself."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The new question word: mistä"
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "color": "grammar",
      "title": "Grammar Insight",
      "text": "Mistä means \"from where.\" The ending -stä signals \"from\" in Finnish. This is your first Finnish case suffix. Don't fear it — just notice it."
    },
    {
      "type": "phrase",
      "annotated": "{Mistä|grammar|from-where|from where} {sä|word|pronoun|you} {oot|grammar|verb|are}?",
      "translation": "Where are you from?"
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "color": "culture",
      "title": "Wait — sä and oot?",
      "text": "Textbooks say sinä (you) and olet (you are). Street Finnish says sä and oot. You're learning street Finnish."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparison",
      "headers": [
        "Colloquial",
        "Formal",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "mä",
          "minä",
          "I"
        ],
        [
          "sä",
          "sinä",
          "you"
        ],
        [
          "oon",
          "olen",
          "I am"
        ],
        [
          "oot",
          "olet",
          "you are"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Answer it — the -stä / -sta ending"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "To say \"from [place]\", add {-stä|grammar|suffix|from} or {-sta|grammar|suffix|from} to the city. Use -stä after front vowels (ä ö y), -sta after back vowels (a o u). Finnish is consistent — this is called vowel harmony."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "fi": "Mä oon {Helsingistä|phrase|ablative|from Helsinki}.",
          "en": "I'm from Helsinki."
        },
        {
          "fi": "Mä oon {Lontoosta|phrase|ablative|from London}.",
          "en": "I'm from London."
        },
        {
          "fi": "Mä oon {Tokiosta|phrase|ablative|from Tokyo}.",
          "en": "I'm from Tokyo."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "dialogue",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "A",
          "fi": "Mistä sä oot?",
          "en": "Where are you from?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "B",
          "fi": "Mä oon Lontoosta. Entä sä?",
          "en": "I'm from London. What about you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "A",
          "fi": "Mä oon Helsingistä.",
          "en": "I'm from Helsinki."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "color": "word",
      "title": "Bonus word — free!",
      "text": "Entä sä? = \"What about you?\" — one of the most useful phrases in any language. You got it for free."
    },
    {
      "type": "pronunciation",
      "content": "Finnish vowels are pure — no diphthongs sneaking in. Hel-sin-gis-tä: four clean syllables. Finnish never swallows syllables the way English does."
    },
    {
      "type": "vocab",
      "rows": [
